-- title:
--   Finiteness of crossings in a field base change of the two-chart model
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a discrete valuation ring (a commutative domain which is a discrete valuation ring), let $K_0$ be a fraction field of $R$, and let $F$ be a field carrying compatible $R$- and $K_0$-algebra structures (a scalar tower $R \to K_0 \to F$). Let $j \in F$ be nonzero (as an instance `Fact (j ≠ 0)`), transcendental over $R$, and assume $F$ is finite-dimensional and separable over the intermediate field $K_0(j) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K₀ {j}`. Let $\varpi \in R$ generate the maximal ideal, so that `IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R = Ideal.span {ϖ}`, and let $\Omega$ be a field with an $R$-algebra structure whose structure map kills the maximal ideal of $R$, i.e. $\Omega$ is an algebra over the residue field of $R$. Write $X =$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel R F j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) for the scheme obtained as the pushout of the two maps $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the inclusions of the project's subalgebras `chartAlgFin R F j` $=$ `chartAlg R F {j}` and `chartAlgInf R F j` $=$ `chartAlg R F {j⁻¹}` of $F$ into the middle ring, and $X \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ for the morphism `toBase` induced on the pushout by the structure morphisms of the two charts. Form the pullback $X \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R} \operatorname{Spec} \Omega$ along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R \to \Omega$, with first projection $\mathrm{pr}$ to $X$. The global section of $X$ obtained by pulling $\varpi$ back through `toBase` on top opens has, at each point $x \in X$, a germ in the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$; the theorem asserts that the set of points $z$ of the pullback for which the ideal generated by this germ at $x = \mathrm{pr}(z)$ does not have a subsingleton set of minimal primes (that is, has at least two distinct minimal primes) is finite.
--
--   This is the version, after an arbitrary field base change $\Omega$ of the residue field, of the statement that the two-chart integral model over a discrete valuation ring has only finitely many "crossing" points, those where the ideal generated by the uniformiser in the local ring has more than one minimal prime. It is used in the comparison of the special fibre of the modular curve $X_1(p)$ with a two-chart integral model, in [`ModularCurve.XOneP.exists_curveModel_pair_hom_specialFibre_birational_twoChartIntegralModel_x1_mul`](thm.html#ModularCurve.XOneP.exists_curveModel_pair_hom_specialFibre_birational_twoChartIntegralModel_x1_mul).
